ded with user and timestamp to /var/log/sudo-commands.log\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Executor configured sudo to log via syslog facility local2 (Defaults syslog=local2 in /etc/sudoers.d/syslog-local2) and routed local2.* to /var/log/sudo-commands.log via rsyslog. Captured log entries (cmd-0db4ef66d38044b9, cmd-d6805cf622b74448) show lines like 'Aug 26 10:39:46 node1 sudo: antrieb : TTY=unknown ; PWD=... ; USER=root ; COMMAND=/bin/whoami', which include a syslog timestamp, the invoking user (antrieb), and the full command. Post-reboot verification (cmd-0002611105664dd5) confirms new commands continue to be logged, showing the configuration is durable.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0db4ef66d38044b9\",\"cmd-d6805cf622b74448\",\"cmd-0002611105664dd5\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"Sudo log entries are kept out of the general system log\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"The rsyslog rule for local2.* discards further processing (~) before the message reaches the catch-all *.info rule that writes /var/log/messages (since /etc/rsyslog.d/*.conf is included earlier in rsyslog.conf than the messages rule). Direct evidence (cmd-9a09c7f21c404c2e, cmd-0813ef04af474c13) shows grep for COMMAND= in /var/log/messages returns only pre-config-change entries; commands run after the rsyslog restart (whoami, id, cat, tail) do not appear there, both before and after the reboot (cmd-0002611105664dd5). This satisfies the conventional reading of 'general system log' as /var/log/messages; the systemd journal itself may still retain these lines since journald receives them independently of rsyslog filtering, which is a plausible but minor interpretation gap rather than a demonstrated failure.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-9a09c7f21c404c2e\",\"cmd-0813ef04af474c13\",\"cmd-0002611105664dd5\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol: pre-restart baseline, acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"cmd-ca44b466335944aa captured the healthy pre-restart baseline (boot_id 16724682..., rsyslog active, 14 log entries). cmd-83f2004d05184353 issued a backgrounded 'shutdown -r now' with an immediate acknowledgement. The node was unreachable mid-reboot (cmd-a210fe652b964380) then returned with boot_id 8a973a0c... (cmd-3d6c0908935048ff), matching the fixed after_executor boot-identity snapshot exactly. cmd-0002611105664dd5 confirms rsyslog active and sudo logging/exclusion from messages both recovered post-restart. This constitutes a full, coherent restart transition.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ca44b466335944aa\",\"cmd-83f2004d05184353\",\"cmd-a210fe652b964380\",\"cmd-3d6c0908935048ff\",\"cmd-0002611105664dd5\"]}],\"confidence\":0.85,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.95,\"summary\":\"All state-changing commands map clearly to task requirements: creating the sudoers drop-in (cmd-b269ff47bfeb4ae5), the rsyslog conf (cmd-976e14fd5c77464b), creating/securing the log file and restarting rsyslog (cmd-3edd4b3605fd47fa), running bounded sudo test commands to generate and verify log entries (cmd-0db4ef66d38044b9, cmd-9a09c7f21c404c2e, cmd-0813ef04af474c13, cmd-d6805cf622b74448, cmd-0002611105664dd5), and the reboot (cmd-83f2004d05184353) performed for the mandated node-restart evidence protocol. 'sudo -k' (inside cmd-0813ef04af474c13) only revokes cached sudo credentials, a trivial and expected side effect of testing, not a harmful mutation. No unnecessary, unreverted, or destructive mutations were found; failed-services and listening-socket snapshots are unchanged aside from expected PID churn from the reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-b269ff47bfeb4ae5\",\"cmd-976e14fd5c77464b\",\"cmd-3edd4b3605fd47fa\",\"cmd-83f2004d05184353\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor correctly implemented sudo command logging to /var/log/sudo-commands.log with user and timestamp, verified isolation from /var/log/messages both before and after a full node restart, and provided a complete, coherent restart-evidence trail (baseline, acknowledgement, changed boot identity, recovery) matching the fixed lifecycle snapshots. No unnecessary or unsafe mutations were introduced.\",\"limitations\":[\"The verification of exclusion from the 'general system log' only checked /var/log/messages; the systemd journal (journalctl) may still independently retain sudo log lines since journald receives them before rsyslog filtering, which was not checked. This is a plausible but unconfirmed interpretation gap rather than an observed failure.\",\"Package-health checks were unavailable (timed out) in both before_prepare and after_executor lifecycle snapshots, so package-state integrity around the reboot could not be independently cross-checked beyond the failed-services and socket listings.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Every sudo command on node1 is recorded with user and timestamp to /var/log/sudo-commands.log","status":"satisfied","summary":"Executor configured sudo to log via syslog facility local2 (Defaults syslog=local2 in /etc/sudoers.d/syslog-local2) and routed local2.* to /var/log/sudo-commands.log via rsyslog. Captured log entries (cmd-0db4ef66d38044b9, cmd-d6805cf622b74448) show lines like 'Aug 26 10:39:46 node1 sudo: antrieb : TTY=unknown ; PWD=... ; USER=root ; COMMAND=/bin/whoami', which include a syslog timestamp, the invoking user (antrieb), and the full command. Post-reboot verification (cmd-0002611105664dd5) confirms new commands continue to be logged, showing the configuration is durable.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0db4ef66d38044b9","cmd-d6805cf622b74448","cmd-0002611105664dd5"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Sudo log entries are kept out of the general system log","status":"satisfied","summary":"The rsyslog rule for local2.* discards further processing (~) before the message reaches the catch-all *.info rule that writes /var/log/messages (since /etc/rsyslog.d/*.conf is included earlier in rsyslog.conf than the messages rule). Direct evidence (cmd-9a09c7f21c404c2e, cmd-0813ef04af474c13) shows grep for COMMAND= in /var/log/messages returns only pre-config-change entries; commands run after the rsyslog restart (whoami, id, cat, tail) do not appear there, both before and after the reboot (cmd-0002611105664dd5). This satisfies the conventional reading of 'general system log' as /var/log/messages; the systemd journal itself may still retain these lines since journald receives them independently of rsyslog filtering, which is a plausible but minor interpretation gap rather than a demonstrated failure.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9a09c7f21c404c2e","cmd-0813ef04af474c13","cmd-0002611105664dd5"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol: pre-restart baseline, acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered state","status":"satisfied","summary":"cmd-ca44b466335944aa captured the healthy pre-restart baseline (boot_id 16724682..., rsyslog active, 14 log entries). cmd-83f2004d05184353 issued a backgrounded 'shutdown -r now' with an immediate acknowledgement. The node was unreachable mid-reboot (cmd-a210fe652b964380) then returned with boot_id 8a973a0c... (cmd-3d6c0908935048ff), matching the fixed after_executor boot-identity snapshot exactly. cmd-0002611105664dd5 confirms rsyslog active and sudo logging/exclusion from messages both recovered post-restart. This constitutes a full, coherent restart transition.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ca44b466335944aa","cmd-83f2004d05184353","cmd-a210fe652b964380","cmd-3d6c0908935048ff","cmd-0002611105664dd5"]}],"confidence":0.85,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.95,"summary":"All state-changing commands map clearly to task requirements: creating the sudoers drop-in (cmd-b269ff47bfeb4ae5), the rsyslog conf (cmd-976e14fd5c77464b), creating/securing the log file and restarting rsyslog (cmd-3edd4b3605fd47fa), running bounded sudo test commands to generate and verify log entries (cmd-0db4ef66d38044b9, cmd-9a09c7f21c404c2e, cmd-0813ef04af474c13, cmd-d6805cf622b74448, cmd-0002611105664dd5), and the reboot (cmd-83f2004d05184353) performed for the mandated node-restart evidence protocol. 'sudo -k' (inside cmd-0813ef04af474c13) only revokes cached sudo credentials, a trivial and expected side effect of testing, not a harmful mutation. No unnecessary, unreverted, or destructive mutations were found; failed-services and listening-socket snapshots are unchanged aside from expected PID churn from the reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b269ff47bfeb4ae5","cmd-976e14fd5c77464b","cmd-3edd4b3605fd47fa","cmd-83f2004d05184353","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"The executor correctly implemented sudo command logging to /var/log/sudo-commands.log with user and timestamp, verified isolation from /var/log/messages both before and after a full node restart, and provided a complete, coherent restart-evidence trail (baseline, acknowledgement, changed boot identity, recovery) matching the fixed lifecycle snapshots. No unnecessary or unsafe mutations were introduced.","limitations":["The verification of exclusion from the 'general system log' only checked /var/log/messages; the systemd journal (journalctl) may still independently retain sudo log lines since journald receives them before rsyslog filtering, which was not checked. This is a plausible but unconfirmed interpretation gap rather than an observed failure.","Package-health checks were unavailable (timed out) in both before_prepare and after_executor lifecycle snapshots, so package-state integrity around the reboot could not be independently cross-checked beyond the failed-services and socket listings."]},"ttft_ms":27503,"type":"result","duration_ms":61989,"uuid":"56ab9572-bf83-4157-a2cf-50d913333699","ttft_stream_ms":1499,"time_to_request_ms":50,"first_content_frame_ms":2012,"queue